 "park_text": "Vapor pressure goes up when temperature goes up. It goes down when temperature goes down. In a cooler liquid, fewer molecules have enough energy to escape.", "status": "approved"}, "reflection": {"state": "reflection", "stage": "close", "label_when_done": "Reflection written", "ask": "Which step was hardest for you? In one sentence, what would you tell a friend who is stuck there?", "status": "approved"}, "dP_found": {"state": "dP_found", "stage": "solve", "label_when_done": "Pressure difference found", "ask": "Now compare the two ends of the bird. How much higher is the pressure in the body than in the head? Type the difference with its unit.", "switch_ask": "You have the pressure in the warm body and the pressure in the cool head. Which is larger, and by how much?", "notebook_prompt": "I need to find the difference between two vapor pressures in a sealed container that is warm at one end and cool at the other. Explain what that difference means and how to find it. Use numbers different from mine. Do not solve my problem. Then ask me one short question and wait for my answer.", "return_ask": "What does the difference between the two pressures tell you?", "park_text": "Subtract: the pressure in the body minus the pressure in the head.", "status": "approved"}, "value_found@P1;P2": {"state": "value_found@P1;P2", "stage": "solve", "label_when_done": "Answer found", "ask": "Carry it through to the pressure you are finding. If you have the natural logarithm of a pressure ratio, what inverse function removes it? Type the pressure with its unit.", "switch_ask": "Look at the prediction pinned on your screen. Does your number agree with it?", "notebook_prompt": "Show me how to solve an equation like ln(x/a) = b for x. Use letters and numbers different from mine. Do not solve my problem. Then give me one short practice problem and wait for my answer.", "return_ask": "What inverse function removes the natural logarithm? And what do you do after that to get the pressure you are finding?", "park_text": "Exponentiate both sides: \\(e^{\\ln(x)} = e^{y}\\), which simplifies to \\(x = e^{y}\\). Here x is the ratio of the two pressures. Then solve that ratio for the pressure you are finding.", "status": "approved (instructor, 10 Oct: the scenarios' texts become the default for pressure problems)"}, "direction_predicted@T1;T2": {"state": "direction_predicted@T1;T2", "stage": "setup", "label_when_done": "Direction predicted", "ask": "Before any math, compare the two pressures. Will the temperature you are finding be higher or lower than the temperature you were given? Pick one.", "switch_ask": "Think about a bubble trying to form. Under more pressure, do the molecules need more energy or less to push it open?", "notebook_prompt": "I need to predict how the boiling point of a liquid changes when the pressure on it changes, before I calculate anything. Explain what the molecules are doing and which way the boiling point goes. Use a liquid I have not named. Do not solve any problem for me. Then ask me one short question and wait for my answer.", "return_ask": "What did the notebook show you about pressure and boiling point?", "park_text": "Boiling point goes up when the pressure goes up. The molecules need more energy to push a bubble open.", "status": "approved"}, "P_from_depth": {"state": "P_from_depth", "stage": "units", "label_when_done": "Pressure at depth found", "ask": "How hard does the water press at the vent? Use the depth to find the pressure. Type it with its unit.", "switch_ask": "Picture the column of water above the vent. Each 10 m of it adds about 1 atm. What does the air above the sea add?", "notebook_prompt": "Explain how the pressure under water grows with depth, and why the air above the water counts too. Use a depth different from mine. Do not solve my problem. Then ask me one short question and wait for my answer.", "return_ask": "What did the notebook show you about pressure and depth?", "park_text": "Pressure = 1 atm from the air + 1 atm for every 10 m of water.", "status": "approved"}, "value_found@T1;T2": {"state": "value_found@T1;T2", "stage": "solve", "label_when_done": "Answer found", "ask": "Carry it through to the temperature. If you have 1/T, what is one more step? Type the temperature with its unit.", "switch_ask": "Look at the prediction pinned on your screen. Does your number agree with it?", "notebook_prompt": "Show me how to solve an equation like 1/x = a - b for x, and how to check that the answer makes sense. Use numbers different from mine. Do not solve my problem. Then give me one short practice problem and wait for my answer.", "return_ask": "What is the last step to get T from 1/T?", "park_text": "Solve for 1/T first. Then take the reciprocal: T = 1 ÷ (your value of 1/T). Convert to °C if the problem asks for it.", "status": "approved (temperature version; archetype default for temperature problems, instructor 10 Oct)"}, "value_found@dH": {"state": "value_found@dH", "stage": "solve", "label_when_done": "Answer found", "ask": "Carry it through to ΔHvap. Type the value with its unit.", "switch_ask": "Does your answer make sense? Most liquids in our table need 25 to 45 kJ/mol to vaporize. Is yours in that range?", "notebook_prompt": "Show me how to solve an equation like ln(a) = -(x/R)(b - c) for x. Use numbers different from mine. Do not solve my problem. Then give me one short practice problem and wait for my answer.", "return_ask": "Which step of the algebra was blocking you?", "park_text": "Divide ln(P2/P1) by (1/T2 - 1/T1). Multiply by -R. Give ΔHvap in kJ/mol.", "status": "approved (ΔHvap version; archetype default for dH problems, instructor 10 Oct)"}}, "moves_by_opener": {"The Syringe Boiling Trick": {"account_given": {"state": "account_given", "stage": "opening", "label_when_done": "Particle account given", "switch_ask": "A pressure gauge inside the syringe would read lower after you pull the plunger. What does that lower pressure let the water molecules do?", "notebook_prompt": "I am learning why a liquid can boil without being heated. Explain what the molecules in a liquid are doing when it boils. Explain why lowering the pressure above a liquid can make it boil. Use a liquid other than water. Do not solve any problem for me. Then ask me one short question and wait for my answer.", "return_ask": "In your own words, what did the notebook show you about why lower pressure lets a liquid boil?", "park_text": "When the external pressure on a liquid drops to its vapor pressure, molecules inside the liquid can form bubbles of vapor. The liquid boils. No heat is needed.", "status": "approved", "show": "Pulling the plunger lowers the external pressure on the water. When the external pressure falls to the water's vapor pressure, molecules inside the liquid have enough energy to push open bubbles of vapor, so the water boils with no heat added. Now say it back in your own words: what are the molecules doing?"}, "meaning_given": {"state": "meaning_given", "stage": "meaning", "label_when_done": "Meaning stated", "ask": "The pressure you found is the pressure inside the syringe at the moment the liquid starts to boil. What does it tell you about the molecules? How sure can you be?", "switch_ask": "We used one value of ΔHvap for every temperature between your two temperatures. If that is not quite true, how far can you trust the pressure you found?", "status": "approved"}, "direction_predicted": {"state": "direction_predicted", "status": "approved (instructor, 12 Oct: show cell)", "show": "At a lower temperature, fewer molecules have enough energy to escape, so the vapor pressure is lower. The pressure you are finding goes with the lower temperature. So: is it higher or lower than the pressure you were given? Pick one."}, "relation_chosen": {"state": "relation_chosen", "status": "approved (instructor, 12 Oct: show cell)", "show": "This problem links two pressures to two temperatures for one liquid. That is the Clausius-Clapeyron equation: ln(P2/P1) = (ΔHvap/R)(1/T1 − 1/T2). Pick it from the Equations list."}, "rearranged": {"state": "rearranged", "status": "approved (instructor, 12 Oct: show cell)", "show": "Call the pressure you are finding P2, and its temperature T2. Exponentiate both sides: \\(e^{\\ln(x)} = e^{y}\\), which simplifies to \\(x = e^{y}\\). Here x is P2/P1. Then multiply by P1: \\(P_2 = P_1\\, e^{(\\Delta H_{vap}/R)(1/T_1 - 1/T_2)}\\). Type it on one line."}, "T_in_K": {"state": "T_in_K", "status": "approved (instructor, 12 Oct: show cell)", "show": "The equation needs kelvin: T in K = T in °C + 273.15. Convert each temperature. Type the values with K."}, "dH_in_J": {"state": "dH_in_J", "status": "approved (instructor, 12 Oct: show cell)", "show": "R is 8.314 J/(mol·K), so ΔHvap must be in J/mol too. Multiply kJ/mol by 1000. Type ΔHvap in J/mol."}, "P_same_units": {"state": "P_same_units", "status": "approved (instructor, 12 Oct: show cell)", "show": "Both pressures must be in the same unit: 1 atm = 760 torr. Convert the one you were given into the unit the problem asks for. Type it with its unit."}, "substituted": {"state": "substituted", "status": "approved (instructor, 12 Oct: show cell)", "show": "Work out the exponent first: (ΔHvap/R)(1/T1 − 1/T2), with ΔHvap in J/mol, R = 8.314 J/(mol·K) and both temperatures in K. Type its value."}, "value_found": {"state": "value_found", "status": "approved (instructor, 12 Oct: show cell)", "show": "Apply the inverse function, \\(e^{x}\\), to that value. That gives P2/P1. Multiply by P1 to get P2. Type P2 with its unit."}}, "The Drinking Bird": {"account_given": {"state": "account_given", "stage": "opening", "label_when_done": "Particle account given", "switch_ask": "A thermometer on the wet head reads a few degrees colder than one on the body. What does the colder glass do to the molecules inside the head?", "notebook_prompt": "I am learning how a drinking bird toy works. Explain what water molecules do when they evaporate from a wet surface. Then explain why a sealed glass container that is cool at one end and warm at the other has a different vapor pressure at each end. Do not solve any problem for me. Then ask me one short question and wait for my answer.", "return_ask": "In your own words, what did the notebook show you about the two ends of the bird?", "park_text": "Water evaporating from the felt cools the head. Inside the glass, the cooler head has a lower vapor pressure than the warm body. The higher pressure in the body pushes liquid up the neck.", "status": "approved"}, "meaning_given": {"state": "meaning_given", "stage": "meaning", "label_when_done": "Meaning stated", "ask": "That difference pushes liquid up the neck. Think of drinking through a straw. In the bird, what acts like your mouth, and what acts like the air pushing down on the drink?", "switch_ask": "Inside the cool head, what are the vapor molecules doing that the ones in the warm body are not?", "status": "approved"}}, "The Deep-Sea Vent": {"account_given": {"state": "account_given", "stage": "opening", "label_when_done": "Particle account given", "switch_ask": "A pressure gauge at the vent reads far higher than at the surface. What must the molecules inside a bubble do to push back against that?", "notebook_prompt": "I am learning why very hot water on the deep sea floor does not boil. Explain what molecules must do to form a bubble of vapor inside a liquid. Explain how the pressure on the liquid changes that. Do not solve any problem for me. Then ask me one short question and wait for my answer.", "return_ask": "In your own words, what did the notebook show you about pressure and bubbles?", "park_text": "A bubble forms only when the vapor inside it pushes back as hard as the liquid around it. Deep water presses hard, so the water must be much hotter before it can boil.", "status": "approved"}, "meaning_given": {"state": "meaning_given", "stage": "meaning", "label_when_done": "Meaning stated", "ask": "Your temperature is where water starts to boil at that depth. What does it tell you about the water molecules there? How sure can you be?", "switch_ask": "We used water's heat of vaporization at 100 °C for every temperature up to yours. How far can you trust the answer at great depth?", "status": "approved"}}, "The Rubbing Alcohol Chill": {"account_given": {"state": "account_given", "stage": "opening", "label_when_done": "Particle account given", "switch_ask": "At the same temperature, a vapor monitor reads much higher over the alcohol than over the water. What does that tell you about how strongly each kind of molecule holds on to its neighbors?", "notebook_prompt": "I am learning why some liquids evaporate faster than others at the same temperature. Explain it in terms of the attractions between molecules. Use liquids other than alcohol and water. Do not solve any problem for me. Then ask me one short question and wait for my answer.", "return_ask": "In your own words, what did the notebook show you about why one liquid evaporates faster than another?", "park_text": "Molecules that hold on to their neighbors less strongly escape into vapor more easily. More of them leave each second, so the liquid dries faster and carries more heat away from your skin.", "status": "approved", "show": "The intermolecular forces between alcohol molecules are weaker than those between water molecules. More alcohol molecules have enough kinetic energy to overcome them and escape, so the alcohol evaporates faster and carries heat away from your skin faster. Now say it back in your own words: what are the molecules doing?"}, "meaning_given": {"state": "meaning_given", "stage": "meaning", "label_when_done": "Meaning stated", "ask": "Your ΔHvap is the energy it takes to pull a mole of the liquid's molecules away from their neighbors and into vapor. What does your number tell you about those molecules? How sure can you be?", "switch_ask": "What did the instrument actually measure and what did you infer from it?", "status": "approved"}, "relation_chosen@v:one": {"state": "relation_chosen", "status": "approved (instructor, 12 Oct: show cell)", "show": "This problem links two vapor pressures and two temperatures for one liquid: the Clausius-Clapeyron equation, \\(\\ln\\dfrac{P_2}{P_1} = \\dfrac{\\Delta H_{vap}}{R}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{T_1} - \\dfrac{1}{T_2}\\right)\\). The normal boiling point is one of your two points: there, the vapor pressure is exactly 1 atm. Pick the equation from the Equations list.", "version": "one"}, "relation_chosen@v:two": {"state": "relation_chosen", "status": "approved (instructor, 12 Oct: show cell)", "show": "This problem links two vapor pressures and two temperatures for one liquid: the Clausius-Clapeyron equation, \\(\\ln\\dfrac{P_2}{P_1} = \\dfrac{\\Delta H_{vap}}{R}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{T_1} - \\dfrac{1}{T_2}\\right)\\). This time both points were measured. Pick the equation from the Equations list.", "version": "two"}, "rearranged": {"state": "rearranged", "status": "approved (instructor, 12 Oct: show cell)", "show": "Solve for \\(\\Delta H_{vap}\\): multiply both sides by R, then divide by the temperature term. \\(\\Delta H_{vap} = \\dfrac{R\\,\\ln(P_2/P_1)}{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{T_1} - \\dfrac{1}{T_2}\\right)}\\). Type it on one line."}, "T_in_K": {"state": "T_in_K", "status": "approved (instructor, 12 Oct: show cell)", "show": "The equation needs kelvin: T in K = T in °C + 273.15. Convert each temperature. Type the values with K."}, "P_same_units@v:one": {"state": "P_same_units", "status": "approved (instructor, 12 Oct: show cell)", "show": "Both pressures must be in the same unit. The boiling-point pressure is 1 atm, which is 760 torr. Convert one so the two match. Type it with its unit.", "version": "one"}, "P_same_units@v:two": {"state": "P_same_units", "status": "approved (instructor, 12 Oct: show cell)", "show": "Both pressures must be in the same unit. Any unit works, as long as the two match. Convert one so they do. Type it with its unit.", "version": "two"}, "substituted": {"state": "substituted", "status": "approved (instructor, 12 Oct: show cell)", "show": "Work out the two pieces first: the natural logarithm term, \\(\\ln(P_2/P_1)\\), which has no unit, and the temperature term, \\(\\left(\\dfrac{1}{T_1} - \\dfrac{1}{T_2}\\right)\\), in 1/K. Type either one."}, "value_found": {"state": "value_found", "status": "approved (instructor, 12 Oct: show cell)", "show": "Multiply the natural logarithm term by R = 8.314 J/(mol·K), then divide by the temperature term. That gives \\(\\Delta H_{vap}\\) in J/mol. Divide by 1000 for kJ/mol. Type it with its unit."}, "offer@v:one": {"state": "offer", "ask": "That problem used the normal boiling point as one of its two points. Would you like to see one worked with the same alcohol, where both points are measured: two temperatures and two vapor pressures?", "status": "approved (instructor, 12 Oct: the offer after the reflection)", "version": "one"}}}, "variants": [{"context": {"substance": "methanol", "one_atm_point": true, "bp_K": 337.75, "lo_K": 277.75, "hi_K": 362.75, "version": "one", "opener": "The Rubbing Alcohol Chill", "template": "Rubbing alcohol cools your skin as it dries. A chemist studies {liquid}. At 1 atm, it boils at {T_b}. At {T}, its vapor pressure is {P}. What is its heat of vaporization? Give it in {unit}.", "items": {"add": [], "remove": []}}, "text": "Rubbing alcohol cools your skin as it dries. A chemist studies a related alcohol. At 1 atm, it boils at 64.6 °C. At 53 °C, its vapor pressure is 0.64 atm. What is its heat of vaporization? Give it in kJ/mol.", "givens": {"T1": {"value": 64.6, "unit": "degC"}, "T2": {"value": 53.0, "unit": "degC"}, "P1": {"value": 760.0, "unit": "torr"}, "P2": {"value": 0.64, "unit": "atm"}}, "sim_params": {"T": "53.0", "T_known": "64.6", "unit": "kJ/mol"}, "board": [{"state": "account_given", "kind": "text", "register": "submicro", "requires": [], "id": "account_given", "stage": "opening", "face": "